     I've never built slash'em, and if it has deviated sufficiently
from nethack then this might not be very helpful.  After using ``make
all'' to build everything, did you use ``make install'' to collect
the built things into the proper directory?  Are you running the new
slash'em in that directory, or the one in the src directory where it
got built.  The latter won't have access to its data files, since
they don't reside in the src directory.

     NetHack has a build-time option called DLB, for data librarian,
to collect the majority of its individual data files into one big
file.  Your slash'em executable was obviously built with that option
enabled, since it is looking for file dungeon in library nhshare.
I don't recall offhand whether the top level Makefile needs to be
told about DLB or whether it figures that out automatically.  If it
needs to be told (edited to specify the DLB configuration) and you
neglected to do that, it would have copied the individual files into
the final directory instead of packing them together and copying the
one big library file, so the program wouldn't find dungeon since it
would be looking for it in a library file rather than in the
directory.

     So that's three possibilities off the top of my head:
1) "make all" but no "make install", so running src/slashem without
data
2) "make all" and "make install", then running src/slashem instead of
  running $NETHACKDIR/slashem so not finding data
3) "make all" and "make install", but slashem and Makefile have
mismatch
  for DLB, so "make install" copied "dungeon" into $NETHACKDIR instead
  of putting it inside "nhshare" and copying the latter there.
No doubt there are other possibilities, but I'm inclined to suspect
either #1 or #2.


